<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George-Étienne Cartier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1814">1814</a>–<a href="/years/1873">1873</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George-Étienne Cartier was a French Canadian lawyer and politician who led the Bleus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada-east">Canada East</a> and shared power with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-a-macdonald">John A. Macdonald</a> in the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. He joined the Great Coalition of 1864 and was a Father of Confederation, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of Canada in 1867. He also championed railway building.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-etienne-cartier.jpg" alt="George-Étienne Cartier" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Notman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_George_Etienne_Cartier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
